(CHAPTER 3: DIAGNOSTIC METRICS & BIAS-VARIANCE TRADEOFF \(Page 3\)) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 3: Model Evaluation · Section 3.1 · Page 3]) Tj T*
(Evaluating classification models requires specialized metrics beyond simple accuracy,) Tj T*
(especially in imbalanced datasets \(e.g., fraud detection with 99% negative class\):) Tj T*
(- Precision = TP / \(TP + FP\): Proportion of predicted positive instances that are) Tj T*
(truly positive. Focuses on minimizing False Positives \(crucial in spam detection\).) Tj T*
(- Recall \(Sensitivity\) = TP / \(TP + FN\): Proportion of actual positive instances) Tj T*
(correctly identified. Focuses on minimizing False Negatives \(critical in disease) Tj T*
(diagnosis\).) Tj T*
(- F1-Score: The harmonic mean of Precision and Recall: F1 = 2 * \(Precision * Recall\) /) Tj T*
(\(Precision + Recall\). The harmonic mean severely penalizes extreme disparities between) Tj T*
(precision and recall compared to the arithmetic mean.) Tj T*
(- Specificity = TN / \(TN + FP\): True negative rate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 3: Model Evaluation · Section 3.2 · Page 3]) Tj T*
(ROC and PR Curves:) Tj T*
(The Receiver Operating Characteristic \(ROC\) curve plots True Positive Rate \(Recall\) vs) Tj T*
(False Positive Rate \(1 - Specificity\) across all discrimination thresholds.) Tj T*
(The Area Under the ROC Curve \(AUC-ROC\) represents the probability that the classifier) Tj T*
(will rank a randomly chosen positive instance higher than a randomly chosen negative) Tj T*
(instance.) Tj T*
(However, for severe class imbalance, ROC curves present an overly optimistic) Tj T*
(assessment because the large number of True Negatives depresses the False Positive) Tj T*
(Rate. The Precision-Recall \(PR\) Curve is strictly preferred for imbalanced data) Tj T*
(evaluation.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([Chapter 3: Model Evaluation · Section 3.3 · Page 3]) Tj T*
(The Bias-Variance Decomposition expresses Expected Test Error as:) Tj T*
(E[Loss] = Bias^2\(f_hat\) + Variance\(f_hat\) + Irreducible Error \(sigma^2\)) Tj T*
(- High Bias \(Underfitting\): The model is too simple to capture underlying patterns) Tj T*
(\(e.g., linear model on quadratic data\). Symptoms: High training error and high test) Tj T*
(error. Remedy: Add features, increase model complexity, decrease regularization.) Tj T*
(- High Variance \(Overfitting\): The model memorizes training noise. Symptoms: Low) Tj T*
(training error but high test error. Remedy: Collect more data, reduce features,) Tj T*
(increase regularization, or use ensemble bagging.) Tj T*
ET
